  "description": "Faithful replication with the original renderings: five bedrooms of 9 m2 designed by architects at the University of Campania L. Vanvitelli, all with the same bed, table, chair, armchair, wardrobe and window onto building facades in the same layout and viewpoint, differing only in colours, materials, textures and furniture style: Basic (white and blue colours, plastic and metal furniture), Modern (white and cold colours, good-quality essential coverings), Comfort (shades of blue and green, velvet coverings), Vivacious (red, blue and green elements, soft-textured materials) and Nature (brown, orange and green colours, wood furniture and leather coverings). Participants give their gender and age, then see the five rooms one at a time in individually randomized order, as in the paper, with the paper's 6-item questionnaire below each picture (Appendix A: How much the IMAGE you just saw makes you feel: calm, nervous, tired, energetic, happy, sad), each on a 9-point scale from 1 = not at all to 9 = extremely. Rebuilt from Rapuano, Sarno, Ruotolo, Ruggiero, Iuliano, Masullo, Maffei, Cioffi & Iachini (2023), Emotional Reactions to Different Indoor Solutions: The Role of Age, Buildings 13(7): 1737, with the renderings cropped from its Figure 1B and the questionnaire from its Appendix A (CC BY 4.0). Image ids: a_basic, b_modern, c_comfort, d_vivacious, e_nature (panels a-e of Figure 1B); answers are in emotions, keyed by image id. Analysis as in the paper: participants grouped by age (younger adults 19-29, adults 30-45, middle-aged adults 46-65, elderly 66 and over) and 4 x 5 mixed ANOVAs on each adjective with age group between and room within subjects, plus age x adjective ANOVAs within each room; the paper (113 participants aged 19-86, online on PsyToolkit, about 10 minutes) found the Nature and Vivacious rooms evoked more happiness and energy and the Basic and Modern rooms more sadness and tiredness, the Basic room more nervousness than the Comfort, Vivacious and Nature rooms, and elderly participants reporting more happiness and energy overall and finding the Basic room less calming. Differences from the original study: (1) the paper recruited by word of mouth and ran on PsyToolkit; here anyone can take part; (2) the gender and age questions and the instruction text are ours; the paper reports gender and age in years; (3) the renderings are the figure copies (about 990 px wide for rooms a-c and 1475 px for rooms d and e, as printed).",
